    "code": "import torch\nimport numpy as np\nfrom torch import nn\nfrom torch.utils.data import TensorDataset, DataLoader\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\n\nclass MyPreprocessor:\n    def __init__(self):\n        pass\n\n    def fit(self, X, y=None):\n        return self\n\n    def transform(self, X):\n        # Reshape the data to separate objects and their features\n        X = X.view(-1, 18, 5)  # 18 objects, 5 features each\n        # Calculate derived features: transverse mass, energy squared, and pseudorapidity squared\n        obj_E = X[:, :, 1]  # Energy\n        obj_pt = X[:, :, 2]  # pT\n        obj_eta = X[:, :, 3]  # eta\n        obj_phi = X[:, :, 4]  # phi\n        obj_px = obj_pt * torch.cos(obj_phi)  # px\n        obj_py = obj_pt * torch.sin(obj_phi)  # py\n        obj_pz = obj_pt * torch.sinh(obj_eta)  # pz\n        X = torch.cat((X[:, :, :1], obj_px.unsqueeze(-1), obj_py.unsqueeze(-1), obj_pz.unsqueeze(-1), X[:, :, 2:]), dim=-1)\n        X = X.view(X.size(0), -1)  # Flatten\n        # Concatenate E_T_miss and phi_{E_t}_miss\n        X = torch.cat((X, X_train[:, :2]), dim=-1)  # Assuming X_train is accessible here, but it should be the input X\n        return X\n\n    def fit_transform(self, X, y=None):\n        self.fit(X, y)\n        return self.transform(X)\n\ndef make_preprocessor():\n    return MyPreprocessor()\n\ndef make_model(input_dim: int):\n    model = nn.Sequential(\n        nn.Linear(input_dim, 128),\n        nn.ReLU(),\n        nn.Dropout(0.2),\n        nn.Linear(128, 64),\n        nn.ReLU(),\n        nn.Dropout(0.2),\n        nn.Linear(64, 1),\n        nn.Sigmoid()\n    )\n    return model\n\nEPOCHS = 10\n\ndef train_model(model, train_loader, val_loader, epochs):\n    criterion = nn.BCELoss()\n    optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.001)\n    train_loss, val_loss, train_acc, val_acc = [], [], [], []\n    for epoch in range(epochs):\n        model.train()\n        total_loss, total_correct = 0, 0\n        for X_batch, y_batch in train_loader:\n            optimizer.zero_grad()\n            outputs = model(X_batch)\n            loss = criterion(outputs, y_batch.float().unsqueeze(-1))\n            loss.backward()\n            optimizer.step()\n            total_loss += loss.item()\n            total_correct += ((outputs > 0.5) == y_batch.unsqueeze(-1)).sum().item()\n        train_loss.append(total_loss / len(train_loader))\n        train_acc.append(total_correct / len(train_loader.dataset))\n        model.eval()\n        val_loss_epoch, val_acc_epoch = 0, 0\n        with torch.no_grad():\n            total_val_correct = 0\n            total_val_loss = 0\n            for X_val_batch, y_val_batch in val_loader:\n                outputs = model(X_val_batch)\n                loss = criterion(outputs, y_val_batch.float().unsqueeze(-1))\n                total_val_loss += loss.item()\n                total_val_correct += ((outputs > 0.5) == y_val_batch.unsqueeze(-1)).sum().item()\n            val_loss_epoch = total_val_loss / len(val_loader)\n            val_acc_epoch = total_val_correct / len(val_loader.dataset)\n        val_loss.append(val_loss_epoch)\n        val_acc.append(val_acc_epoch)\n    return model, train_loss, val_loss, train_acc, val_acc\n",
    "explanation": "The provided code implements a binary classification model using PyTorch to maximize the AUC for a particle physics dataset. The data is pre-processed to extract meaningful features from the given object properties and missing transverse energy. The model is a simple neural network with two hidden layers and dropout for regularization. The training loop is defined with a binary cross-entropy loss function and Adam optimizer. The model's performance is evaluated on both the training and validation sets during training."
}
