    "code": "import torch\nimport numpy as np\nfrom torch import nn\nfrom torch.utils.data import TensorDataset, DataLoader\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\n\nclass MyPreprocessor:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.max_objects = 18\n        self.object_features = 5\n        self.feature_names = ['E_T_miss', 'phi_{E_t}_miss']\n        for i in range(self.max_objects):\n            self.feature_names.extend([f'obj_{i+1}', f'E_{i+1}', f'p_T{i+1}', f'eta_{i+1}', f'phi_{i+1}'])\n\n    def fit(self, X, y=None):\n        return self\n\n    def transform(self, X):\n        # Reshape the data to separate objects and their properties\n        X_objects = X[:, 2:].view(-1, self.max_objects, self.object_features)\n        # Sort objects by p_T in descending order\n        sorted_indices = torch.argsort(X_objects[:, :, 2], dim=1, descending=True)\n        X_objects = torch.gather(X_objects, 1, sorted_indices.unsqueeze(-1).expand(-1, -1, self.object_features))\n        X_objects = X_objects.view(-1, self.max_objects * self.object_features)\n        # Concatenate E_T_miss and phi_{E_t}_miss with the sorted object features\n        X_transformed = torch.cat((X[:, :2], X_objects), dim=1)\n        return X_transformed\n\n    def fit_transform(self, X, y=None):\n        self.fit(X, y)\n        return self.transform(X)\n\ndef make_preprocessor():\n    return MyPreprocessor()\n\ndef make_model(input_dim: int):\n    model = nn.Sequential(\n        nn.Linear(input_dim, 128),\n        nn.ReLU(),\n        nn.Dropout(0.2),\n        nn.Linear(128, 64),\n        nn.ReLU(),\n        nn.Dropout(0.2),\n        nn.Linear(64, 1),\n        nn.Sigmoid()\n    )\n    return model\n\nEPOCHS = 10\n\ndef train_model(model, train_loader, val_loader, epochs):\n    criterion = nn.BCELoss()\n    optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.001)\n    train_loss, val_loss, train_acc, val_acc = [], [], [], []\n    for epoch in range(epochs):\n        model.train()\n        total_loss, total_correct = 0, 0\n        for X_batch, y_batch in train_loader:\n            optimizer.zero_grad()\n            outputs = model(X_batch)\n            loss = criterion(outputs, y_batch.unsqueeze(1).float())\n            loss.backward()\n            optimizer.step()\n            total_loss += loss.item()\n            total_correct += ((outputs > 0.5).int() == y_batch.unsqueeze(1)).sum().item()\n        train_loss.append(total_loss / len(train_loader))\n        train_acc.append(total_correct / len(train_loader.dataset))\n        model.eval()\n        total_loss, total_correct = 0, 0\n        with torch.no_grad():\n            for X_batch, y_batch in val_loader:\n                outputs = model(X_batch)\n                loss = criterion(outputs, y_batch.unsqueeze(1).float())\n                total_loss += loss.item()\n                total_correct += ((outputs > 0.5).int() == y_batch.unsqueeze(1)).sum().item()\n        val_loss.append(total_loss / len(val_loader))\n        val_acc.append(total_correct / len(val_loader.dataset))\n    return model, train_loss, val_loss, train_acc, val_acc",
    "explanation": "This solution starts by defining a preprocessor that sorts the objects in each event by their transverse momentum (p_T) in descending order. This is a common technique in particle physics to give more importance to the objects with higher p_T. The preprocessor concatenates the missing transverse energy (E_T_miss) and its azimuthal angle (phi_{E_t}_miss) with the sorted object features.\n\nThe model is a simple neural network with two hidden layers, using ReLU activation and dropout for regularization. The output layer uses sigmoid activation to produce probabilities.\n\nThe training loop uses binary cross-entropy loss and Adam optimizer. It tracks the loss and accuracy on both the training and validation sets during training.\n\nThe code is designed to be efficient and meet the runtime constraints. It avoids large space searches and extensive hyperparameter tuning."
}
